A||||3||BENGAL4||Fuel Injectors
Z||000000||BENGAL4||08-11-99||09:24 PM||||After painting my rocker arm covers and plenum, I was putting everything back together including putting the fuel injectors back into their respective holes and some are seating deeper than others. The fuel rail is flush along with the upper intake manifold. Is there some trick to seating them deeper without taking them off of the fuel rail. The ones in question are the two in the middle on the firewall side of the engine. They are in the holes but you can still seea little bit of the O ring. The other four are seated ok. HELP. 
Z||000001||cmyokley||08-12-99||07:56 AM||||Bengal4,<br>This might not help but I do know that all O-Rings, of a specific size, are not always exactly the same size. There is a tolerence built in that can make the o-ring have a different "squeeze" in the bore it is going into. Therefore there could be more drag on certain injectors than the one right next to it making it harder to get in. Did you put a little lubricant on the o-rings?<p>I don't think in the long run there would be a problem if one injector was a little higher than the other and there should not be a problem with sealing. You might try pushing a little harder on the problem injertors, if you have not already tried that.
Z||000002||theogre||08-12-99||02:40 PM||||did you remember to put some light grease or tranny fluid on the o rings before stuffing them in the holes? That could make them bind if not.
Z||000003||terryk||08-13-99||08:16 AM||||Vaseline.<p>Once the rail is bolted down, you might see a bit of the o-ring in the intake. This assumes they are seated in the fuel rail correctly.
